<Page
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="How Antarctical handles your Google account and calendar data."
  updated="28 October 2026"
>
  <p>
    This policy explains how Antarctical, a desktop calendar app for Linux, handles
    your information. Antarctical is provided by P4X-639 Ltd, trading as Rawkode
    Academy ("we", "us").
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>In short:</strong> Antarctical runs on your computer and talks to Google
    directly. Your Google data is never sent to us or to anyone else. We do not
    collect analytics, telemetry or crash reports.
  </div>

  <h2>Google user data Antarctical accesses</h2>
  <p>When you sign in with Google, Antarctical asks for these permissions:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Permission</th><th>What Antarctical does with it</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>openid</code>, <code>userinfo.email</code>, <code>userinfo.profile</code></td>
        <td>Identifies the signed-in account, so you can tell your accounts apart in the app.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>calendar</code></td>
        <td>
          Reads your calendar list and events (titles, times, locations, descriptions,
          guests, video-call links and reminders) to display them. Editing features
          use it to create, change or delete events and calendars, and only when you
          ask Antarctical to.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>calendar.app.created</code></td>
        <td>Manages calendars that Antarctical creates on your behalf, when you ask it to.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Antarctical uses this data only to provide its calendar features to you. It does
    not use it for advertising, does not sell it, and does not use it to develop or
    train AI or machine-learning models.
  </p>

  <h2>Where your data is kept</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar data</strong> is held in memory while Antarctical runs and is
      not written to disk.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in tokens</strong> (one refresh token per Google account) and, if you
      choose to use your own OAuth client, its ID and secret are stored in your
      desktop's keyring (Secret Service: GNOME Keyring, KWallet, KeePassXC or
      similar). Short-lived access tokens are kept in memory only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Preferences</strong> (which calendars are visible, the selected view,
      reminders, clock format and whether declined events are shown) are saved in
      <code>~/.config/antarctical/settings.json</code> on your computer.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sharing</h2>
  <p>
    Antarctical exchanges data only with Google, over encrypted connections, to sign
    you in and to read or change your calendars. We do not receive, store or share
    your Google data. On your own computer, Antarctical shows desktop notifications
    for reminders and, if you use the Dux desktop shell, makes your events available
    to it over a local socket on your computer.
  </p>

  <h2>Google API Services User Data Policy</h2>
  <p>
    Antarctical's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs to any
    other app will adhere to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
    including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>Removing access and deleting data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Removing an account in Antarctical's Settings revokes its access with Google
      and deletes its token from your keyring.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can also revoke access at any time from your
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">Google Account permissions</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Uninstalling Antarctical and deleting <code>~/.config/antarctical</code> and its
      keyring items removes everything it stored. As we hold none of your data,
      there is nothing for us to delete.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This website is hosted by Cloudflare, which processes technical information such
    as IP addresses to deliver and secure it. The website sets no cookies and uses
    no analytics.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under UK data protection law you have rights over your personal data, including
    access, correction and deletion. Because Antarctical keeps your data on your own
    computer, you can exercise most of these directly. For anything else, or if you
    have a question about this policy, email
    <a href="mailto:david@rawkode.academy">david@rawkode.academy</a>. You can also
    complain to the <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">Information Commissioner's Office</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>Antarctical is not directed at children under 13.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, we will update it here and change the date above. If a
    change affects how Antarctical uses your Google data, we will ask for your
    consent where required.
  </p>
</Page>
